/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An optical node does more than join fibers.
   - An optical cross-connect (OXC) switches many wavelengths between many input and output fibers, so it can rearrange lightpaths across a mesh.
   - Size first.
   - In broadcast-and-select, each input fiber is split into as many copies as there are output fibers.
   - Millisecond elements suit circuit switching of lightpaths; nanosecond elements are needed for burst and packet switching.
   - A lightpath keeps one wavelength from source to destination unless a converter changes it.
   - Si is the set of free wavelengths on link i; k is the number of links; S is the set of wavelengths free on every link.
   - Checking only neighbouring pairs of links is wrong: every link must share the wavelength.
   - Routing and wavelength assignment (RWA) picks a path and a wavelength for each lightpath.
   - Protection reuses the ring ideas of SONET/SDH (UPSR and BLSR) and the mesh schemes 1+1 and shared backup paths; an OXC speeds restoration by switching whole wavelengths.
*/
